          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have stage 3 kidney disease and high blood pressure. Does the classification link the two, and what target does guidance describe for someone like me?",
          "answer": "**Does the classification link the two?** Yes, but only in the coding system. The ICD-10 coding guide (coding-hypertension-i10) says the classification presumes a causal link between hypertension and chronic kidney disease and pairs them automatically. This is a rule for how medical records are coded. It does not tell us what caused your kidney disease or whether your blood pressure is related to it.\n\n**What target applies to you?** The passages I retrieved do not give a blood-pressure target for people with stage 3 kidney disease, so I can't give you one from these sources. The only target they state is from the coronary artery disease guidance (coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention), which says below 130/80 mm Hg for most people with that heart condition. That is not guidance about kidney disease, so it should not be read as your target. The hypertension guidance (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment) says that where chronic kidney disease is present, a class of blood-pressure medicines called renin-angiotensin blockers is preferred. It does not name a target number.\n\nYour target and treatment depend on your full picture, including any protein in your urine, your age, and your other conditions. Please ask your doctor or kidney team what blood-pressure goal fits you and whether your current plan matches it. Do not change any medicine on your own.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
